1
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:09,957
The world.

2
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:11,877
What a glorious place.

3
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:16,117
Seek freedom, and it will lie
stretched out before your eyes.

4
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:19,340
If the endless dream guides
your restless spirit,

5
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:23,960
seize it, raise your flag, and stand tall.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,717
I think back to the time
when my search first began.

7
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:35,297
I left behind those shores of sand.

8
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:38,700
For the endless everlasting
world out there.

9
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:42,817
I know that with the
burning passion driving me.

10
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:48,540
Someday, somewhere, I'll finally see the
light I have searched for everywhere.

11
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:55,757
When the summer sun is shining.

12
00:00:55,840 --> 00:00:58,077
Shaking the sails of my heart.

13
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:02,100
But I know the way the long moon's sea.

14
00:01:03,020 --> 00:01:05,817
A brand new world that I have been hiding.

15
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:08,797
Has opened up its doors for me to go.

16
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:11,900
The way the long moon's sky.

17
00:01:13,020 --> 00:01:15,357
Wearing in the troubled waves.

18
00:01:15,440 --> 00:01:17,877
From all despair I can be saved.

19
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:21,177
For the horizon on the other side is.

20
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:28,617
And though I travel far
from where my search began.

21
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:31,177
Over seas of green and shores of sand.

22
00:01:31,260 --> 00:01:34,580
Through the endless
everlasting world out there.

23
00:01:35,420 --> 00:01:38,717
I know that with this
burning passion driving me.

24
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:41,857
Some days somewhere I'll
finally see the light.

25
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:44,420
I will search for everywhere.

26
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:56,653
What did you want to tell us, old-timer?

27
00:01:56,820 --> 00:01:58,349
Listen up and listen good.

28
00:01:58,620 --> 00:02:02,679
I've decided to tell you guys
everything I know about the Sky Island.

29
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Most of this is rumor and hearsay,

30
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:08,137
so you'll have to make up your own
minds whether you believe it or not.

31
00:02:08,220 --> 00:02:09,700
I believe it. Quiet!

32
00:02:10,340 --> 00:02:13,857
There's something very strange that
happens in this part of the ocean.

33
00:02:13,940 --> 00:02:16,237
Everything will suddenly
turn black as night.

34
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:18,137
Right in the middle of the brightest day!

35
00:02:18,220 --> 00:02:21,320
Hey, yeah, the same thing
happened to us, right?

36
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:23,517
Yeah, it was like the sun vanished.

37
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:26,129
And then these enormous
monsters showed up.

38
00:02:27,960 --> 00:02:29,297
You saw the giants.

39
00:02:29,380 --> 00:02:31,257
There's a tale about where they come from.

40
00:02:31,340 --> 00:02:32,537
But forget about them for now.

41
00:02:32,620 --> 00:02:33,577
We can go into it later.

42
00:02:33,660 --> 00:02:36,080
Now about this strange nightfall.

43
00:02:38,860 --> 00:02:42,060
It's caused by the shadow
of an extremely dense cloud.

44
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:44,437
Cumulonimbus clouds?

45
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:46,277
But it was too dark for clouds.

46
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:47,477
It was like the sun went out.

47
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:49,560
Yeah, it was more of an eclipse.

48
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:52,177
Clouds on a cloudy day
are just sort of cloudy.

49
00:02:52,260 --> 00:02:53,517
Yeah, just sort of cloudy.

50
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:54,430
Yeah, cloudy!

51
00:02:54,513 --> 00:02:56,029
Shut your gums and listen!

52
00:02:56,340 --> 00:02:58,800
This cloud is called a cumuloregalus.

53
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,882
It forms high in the sky, it has no internal
air currents, and never turns to rain.

54
00:03:03,980 --> 00:03:08,333
No light can reach the ground, and a
sunny afternoon turns black as pitch.

55
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:13,037
It's like some giant hand reached up and
snuffed out the Sun like it was a candle.

56
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:16,680
Some say the cumuloregalus
clouds are floating fossils?

57
00:03:17,220 --> 00:03:21,157
Petrified clouds that hang in the
sky unchanged for thousands of years.

58
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:26,620
You've got to be kidding us a Petrified
cloud that drifts around for centuries. Hey,

59
00:03:26,740 --> 00:03:28,617
I'm just telling you
what people have told me

60
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:31,697
whether you believe it or
not Is entirely up to you.

61
00:03:31,780 --> 00:03:33,780
So it's a mystery cloud.

62
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:37,469
Yeah, I guess that sums it up
about as well as anything else

63
00:03:38,660 --> 00:03:41,697
You see there's only one
place this sky island can be

64
00:03:41,780 --> 00:03:45,297
if it's real and that's on top
of one of those clouds. Yeah

65
00:03:45,380 --> 00:03:52,380
that's

66
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:01,830
gonna be the

67
00:04:01,913 --> 00:04:02,743
hard part because you'll

68
00:04:02,826 --> 00:04:05,558
be risking your lives every
minute you mean like now!

69
00:04:05,780 --> 00:04:08,997
There's only one way to get
there, the knock-up stream.

70
00:04:09,080 --> 00:04:10,737
It shoots straight up in the air,

71
00:04:10,820 --> 00:04:13,977
so it could theoretically take
you into the sky if you're lucky.

72
00:04:14,060 --> 00:04:14,890
Wait a minute.

73
00:04:14,973 --> 00:04:17,757
Are you trying to say this current
can actually send ships flying?

74
00:04:17,840 --> 00:04:18,997
Great, then it'll be a snap.

75
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:21,198
All we have to do is hit to ride up.

76
00:04:21,900 --> 00:04:22,730
Yahoo!

77
00:04:24,080 --> 00:04:25,320
On an ocean current?

78
00:04:25,780 --> 00:04:27,737
Except wouldn't we wind
up falling back into

79
00:04:27,820 --> 00:04:30,291
the ocean and getting smashed into pieces?

80
00:04:30,440 --> 00:04:33,617
Usually, that's true, unless you
can get the timing just right.

81
00:04:33,700 --> 00:04:38,288
It's one thing to sit here all comfy and
talk about riding the knock-up stream

82
00:04:38,620 --> 00:04:41,777
But if you think this trip is going
to be some kind of pleasure cruise,

83
00:04:41,860 --> 00:04:43,919
you're in for a very rude awakening

84
00:04:45,700 --> 00:04:48,759
The knock-up stream is as
dangerous as any hurricane

85
00:04:48,980 --> 00:04:51,160
It should be avoided at all costs

86
00:04:53,240 --> 00:04:57,177
But how exactly can a current of water
rise up in the air in the first place?

87
00:04:57,260 --> 00:05:01,084
Come on, we've never heard anything
like that before, right guys?

88
00:05:03,900 --> 00:05:07,217
I've heard a lot of different
explanations for the knock-up stream,

89
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:09,677
but there's only been one
that's made any sense to me,

90
00:05:09,760 --> 00:05:13,557
though so far no one has been
stupid enough to try and confirm it.

91
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:14,900
It's like this.

92
00:05:18,860 --> 00:05:22,919
Deep below the ocean floor, there
are large cavities filled with air.

93
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:25,797
Cool seawater seeps
into these air pockets,

94
00:05:25,880 --> 00:05:29,056
and geothermal energy heats
it until it becomes steam.

95
00:05:29,460 --> 00:05:31,237
Over time, the pressure builds,

96
00:05:31,320 --> 00:05:35,438
And when it finally becomes too
great, there's a tremendous explosion.

97
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:40,369
This explosion hurls the water into the
air and creates a current to the sky.

98
00:05:41,120 --> 00:05:43,480
The effect lasts for about one minute,

99
00:05:43,780 --> 00:05:47,280
connecting the ocean to the
sky like a bridge. One minute?

100
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:50,997
Do you realize how enormous an
explosion like that would have to be?

101
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:55,317
The explosions occur in widely different
locations about five times a month.

102
00:05:55,400 --> 00:05:58,576
We'd be blown into fish food
if we tried that old man.

103
00:05:59,100 --> 00:06:03,477
Anyway, you can't really explain something
like this with words and theories.

104
00:06:03,560 --> 00:06:05,657
It's one of the great
mysteries of creation.

105
00:06:05,740 --> 00:06:06,900
So, so then,

106
00:06:07,040 --> 00:06:09,157
we gotta ride this current
that shows up five times

107
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:11,557
a month into the air to
reach some island in the sky?

108
00:06:11,640 --> 00:06:13,960
And just hope it's waiting for us?

109
00:06:14,140 --> 00:06:16,258
I'd say that sums it up pretty well.

110
00:06:16,600 --> 00:06:18,980
And if you miss, you fall back down,

111
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:22,161
slam into the ocean, and
get blown into fish food.

112
00:06:22,720 --> 00:06:23,900
Just like you said.

113
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:30,555
Okay, so we're going to forget
about Sky Island, right?

114
00:06:31,980 --> 00:06:33,197
Because it's nuts, Loopy.

115
00:06:33,280 --> 00:06:34,277
It's completely insane.

116
00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:35,557
You have to be luckier than Mr.

117
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:40,346
Lucky McLuckluck of Lucky Land on its
lucky day to pull off a set like this one.

118
00:06:41,940 --> 00:06:44,600
It'll be okay. Let's go. Naturally.

119
00:06:44,960 --> 00:06:46,437
How can you say it'll be okay?

120
00:06:46,520 --> 00:06:47,837
Think about it for a second.

121
00:06:47,920 --> 00:06:49,797
And what about the poor Going Merry?

122
00:06:49,880 --> 00:06:52,117
Did you even stop to
consider the shape she's in?

123
00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:53,817
The way she's suffering.

124
00:06:53,900 --> 00:06:56,117
There's no way you could
expect her to stand

125
00:06:56,200 --> 00:06:58,157
up to a disaster like
this in her condition!

126
00:06:58,240 --> 00:07:00,237
He's absolutely right, she's worthless.

127
00:07:00,320 --> 00:07:04,040
Even brand new, that wreck
wouldn't stand a chance. Say what?

128
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:06,217
Speed, weight, strength.

129
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:11,241
The explosion would blow that ship to pieces
before it had a chance to get airborne!

130
00:07:12,120 --> 00:07:14,220
Yeah, see, it's hopeless.

131
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:17,217
But don't worry, I think
I can help you out there.

132
00:07:17,300 --> 00:07:20,417
I'll have Mashida and Shoujo do
a complete refit of your ship.

133
00:07:20,500 --> 00:07:23,937
I'll also have them reinforce the
superstructure to withstand the strain.

134
00:07:24,020 --> 00:07:24,850
You bet!

135
00:07:24,933 --> 00:07:26,817
Don't worry, guys, we're all over it!

136
00:07:26,900 --> 00:07:27,782
Thanks, fellas!

137
00:07:28,320 --> 00:07:29,640
Yeah, thanks a lot.

138
00:07:31,820 --> 00:07:33,517
Have you really thought about this?

139
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:34,430
What?

140
00:07:34,513 --> 00:07:35,343
Well, about...

141
00:07:35,426 --> 00:07:37,077
Oh, yeah, how about the fact that at most,

142
00:07:37,160 --> 00:07:40,717
we can only stay one more day on this
island, or did that slip your mind?

143
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:41,630
After that,

144
00:07:41,713 --> 00:07:45,657
the Log Pose will lock on to the next
island's location and we'll have to move on.

145
00:07:45,740 --> 00:07:47,420
That's right! That's right!

146
00:07:47,620 --> 00:07:48,537
We gotta move on!

147
00:07:48,620 --> 00:07:49,740
Hey, old man.

148
00:07:49,980 --> 00:07:53,457
I realize you're not a fortune teller and
you probably don't have the vaguest idea.

149
00:07:53,540 --> 00:07:55,457
But how many days will it
be until the next time the

150
00:07:55,540 --> 00:07:57,457
knock-up stream lines up
with the cumulary gallus?

151
00:07:57,540 --> 00:07:58,617
Or better yet, how many weeks?

152
00:07:58,700 --> 00:07:59,530
Or months?

153
00:07:59,613 --> 00:08:00,443
Or years?

154
00:08:00,526 --> 00:08:01,356
Or centuries?

155
00:08:01,439 --> 00:08:02,269
Now take your time.

156
00:08:02,352 --> 00:08:03,182
I'm in no hurry.

157
00:08:03,265 --> 00:08:04,095
Can I lend you an abacus?

158
00:08:04,178 --> 00:08:06,917
It'll be at around noon tomorrow,
so you better get ready.

159
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:07,830
Wait a minute!

160
00:08:07,913 --> 00:08:08,897
You mean we can make it?

161
00:08:08,980 --> 00:08:09,810
Afraid so.

162
00:08:09,893 --> 00:08:11,762
If you're scared, then don't go.

163
00:08:14,140 --> 00:08:14,970
You're lying.

164
00:08:15,980 --> 00:08:17,677
There's something fishy about this.

165
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:19,597
We've known you less than 24 hours.

166
00:08:19,680 --> 00:08:21,337
Why are you being so helpful?

167
00:08:21,420 --> 00:08:22,637
And what's more...

168
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:23,840
Now hold on, Uso.

169
00:08:23,960 --> 00:08:25,737
Ah, you hold up till I'm finished.

170
00:08:25,820 --> 00:08:29,817
This Sky Island, this legendary place
we don't even know the location of,

171
00:08:29,900 --> 00:08:33,140
is just gonna show up out of
the blue at noon tomorrow.

172
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:37,037
And to help us get there, you're
gonna rebuild and reinforce our ship.

173
00:08:37,120 --> 00:08:37,950
For nothing?

174
00:08:38,340 --> 00:08:41,437
When something looks too good
to be true, it probably is.

175
00:08:41,520 --> 00:08:44,777
The infamous liar Nolan is your
great-great-grand whatever, right?

176
00:08:44,860 --> 00:08:47,180
So how do we know we can trust you?

177
00:08:49,740 --> 00:08:52,580
Hey, old man! Soup's on! Come and get him!

178
00:08:53,160 --> 00:08:54,100
Move it!

179
00:08:54,820 --> 00:08:55,650
Nami!

180
00:08:59,220 --> 00:09:00,420
What is it?

181
00:09:00,700 --> 00:09:01,935
You want to fight me?

182
00:09:02,680 --> 00:09:06,320
Mashida said, Whenever the
midday night occurs in this area,

183
00:09:06,720 --> 00:09:10,485
