in the southern sky the next day.

184
00:09:11,440 --> 00:09:14,437
Based on its usual cycle of
five appearances a month,

185
00:09:14,520 --> 00:09:18,317
I'm guessing the knock-up stream
will also erupt tomorrow at midday.

186
00:09:18,400 --> 00:09:20,753
That will probably also be to the south.

187
00:09:20,840 --> 00:09:25,605
I can't be 100 % certain, but there's a
good chance the two events will coincide.

188
00:09:32,920 --> 00:09:36,120
I'm proud to have met a bunch
of brave fools like you.

189
00:09:36,340 --> 00:09:37,640
You're true pirates.

190
00:09:38,900 --> 00:09:41,300
Now then, let's go inside and eat.

191
00:09:41,700 --> 00:09:44,347
Tonight I want you to
call my home your home.

192
00:09:44,520 --> 00:09:45,350
My friends.

193
00:09:48,800 --> 00:09:50,624
Come on, Usopp, it's chow time!

194
00:09:50,840 --> 00:09:52,252
Yeah, come on, you guys!

195
00:09:52,800 --> 00:09:54,094
Chopper, go get Robin.

196
00:09:56,980 --> 00:10:01,274
It looks like we're going to this Sky
Island, whether we're ready or not.

197
00:10:01,560 --> 00:10:03,957
Well, let's just hope our
luck continues to hold.

198
00:10:04,040 --> 00:10:07,180
Nami, do you think I'm a
spineless little coward?

199
00:10:07,420 --> 00:10:09,067
You forgot to mention idiot.

200
00:10:09,200 --> 00:10:10,612
And I know how you feel.

201
00:10:11,080 --> 00:10:12,551
Now why not go apologize?

202
00:10:15,340 --> 00:10:18,060
I'm sorry, old-timer. What are you doing?

203
00:10:18,420 --> 00:10:20,008
Don't wipe that nose on me!

204
00:10:20,240 --> 00:10:21,940
It's disgusting! Idiot!

205
00:10:24,620 --> 00:10:27,091
Come over here, buddy, and get in with me!

206
00:10:27,740 --> 00:10:29,340
Oh, yeah, keep it coming.

207
00:10:29,520 --> 00:10:30,917
That really hits the old spot.

208
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:31,830
Eat up, everyone.

209
00:10:31,913 --> 00:10:33,177
I'm just getting started.

210
00:10:33,260 --> 00:10:35,084
There's plenty more on the way.

211
00:10:36,300 --> 00:10:38,080
Gotcha, face! Oh, yeah!

212
00:10:38,360 --> 00:10:39,780
It was hot sauce!

213
00:10:40,640 --> 00:10:43,117
Come here, you squirrely
godly little face!

214
00:10:43,200 --> 00:10:44,237
Hey, good-looking.

215
00:10:44,320 --> 00:10:45,317
Want to sit next to me?

216
00:10:45,400 --> 00:10:46,757
Whoa, shift down the gear, old man.

217
00:10:46,840 --> 00:10:50,311
You're about a hundred years
too old to be hitting on Nani.

218
00:10:58,040 --> 00:10:59,420
The skull's right eye.

219
00:11:00,480 --> 00:11:02,127
That's where I saw the gold.

220
00:11:07,240 --> 00:11:11,711
Those words smeared with tears are the
last ones Nolan wrote in his logbook.

221
00:11:12,060 --> 00:11:15,060
The skull's right eye,
that's where I saw the gold.

222
00:11:16,020 --> 00:11:18,760
That very same day, Noland was executed!

223
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:21,637
I still don't know what he meant by that?

224
00:11:21,720 --> 00:11:24,457
Does it refer to the
name of a city or some

225
00:11:24,540 --> 00:11:26,997
landmark that used to exist on the island?

226
00:11:27,080 --> 00:11:30,551
Or is it just a poetic allusion
to his own impending death?

227
00:11:30,760 --> 00:11:33,440
The blank pages that
follow give us no clue.

228
00:11:35,280 --> 00:11:38,477
And that's the reason we continue
to dive to the bottom of the sea!

229
00:11:38,560 --> 00:11:39,860
We dream.

230
00:11:41,160 --> 00:11:42,440
Hooray for us!

231
00:11:44,820 --> 00:11:46,600
We're gonna go flying!

232
00:11:47,760 --> 00:11:50,289
Not if you keep eating
like that, we won't!

233
00:11:55,220 --> 00:11:58,577
It's getting pretty dark, but I
can still make out the shoreline.

234
00:11:58,660 --> 00:11:59,490
It's clear.

235
00:11:59,573 --> 00:12:01,560
Drop anchor and prepare to land!

236
00:12:01,880 --> 00:12:06,200
Golden treasure shouldn't be
wasted on starry-eyed dreamers.

237
00:12:08,520 --> 00:12:11,557
Log Entry for May City, 1122.

238
00:12:11,640 --> 00:12:13,860
Today I Arrived on Jaya.

239
00:12:14,980 --> 00:12:16,920
Shortly after making landfall,

240
00:12:17,060 --> 00:12:18,937
we were startled by the peculiar cries of

241
00:12:19,020 --> 00:12:20,957
some strange bird from
deep in the forest!.

242
00:12:21,040 --> 00:12:24,117
As well as the tolling of what
seemed to be an enormous bell.

243
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:28,017
The ringing of that golden
bell resounded far and wide!.

244
00:12:28,100 --> 00:12:32,297
As if announcing the presence of some
great and prosperous city of the past.

245
00:12:32,380 --> 00:12:35,137
Its fleeting echoes the
only reminder that this

246
00:12:35,220 --> 00:12:38,140
land was once home to
a mighty civilization.

247
00:12:41,360 --> 00:12:43,477
No land, no land?.

248
00:12:43,560 --> 00:12:44,857
In our pride and arrogance,

249
00:12:44,940 --> 00:12:47,537
we thought we had seen and heard
everything in our short lives!.

250
00:12:47,620 --> 00:12:50,557
But the sound of that bell
tolling left us all speechless!.

251
00:12:50,640 --> 00:12:54,816
It was as if the clang of that bell
had struck us all dumb with wonder.

252
00:13:00,100 --> 00:13:02,197
I just love stories with bells?.

253
00:13:02,280 --> 00:13:03,697
and them specially gold ones.

254
00:13:03,780 --> 00:13:08,604
Hey, old-timer, it sounds like you just
might be a little fond of Nolan after all.

255
00:13:09,460 --> 00:13:10,420
Look at this.

256
00:13:10,560 --> 00:13:12,120
Wow, a golden bell.

257
00:13:13,300 --> 00:13:15,680
Huh, doesn't seem too enormous to me.

258
00:13:16,280 --> 00:13:19,617
None of these is the one Nolan
was writing about in his log book.

259
00:13:19,700 --> 00:13:23,337
These are bell-shaped golden ingots we
found on the ocean floor during a dive.

260
00:13:23,420 --> 00:13:24,250
Golden ingot?

261
00:13:24,333 --> 00:13:25,560
The city of gold! Ha!

262
00:13:25,720 --> 00:13:26,560
It is real.

263
00:13:26,820 --> 00:13:28,526
It's still not proof, though.

264
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,137
You could find golden
relics like that lying

265
00:13:31,220 --> 00:13:33,417
around in any old ruin in
this part of the world.

266
00:13:33,500 --> 00:13:37,897
Maybe so, but it does suggest there was
a civilization here sometime in the past.

267
00:13:37,980 --> 00:13:41,257
Ingots are made to divide gold
into standardized units of weight.

268
00:13:41,340 --> 00:13:44,177
And that would indicate an
advanced trading society.

269
00:13:44,260 --> 00:13:45,090
Right.

270
00:13:45,173 --> 00:13:47,997
And you remember that peculiar bird call
he said they heard when they landed?

271
00:13:48,080 --> 00:13:49,021
Show it to them.

272
00:13:51,500 --> 00:13:52,537
There's more gold!

273
00:13:52,620 --> 00:13:54,257
Just look at the size of it.

274
00:13:54,340 --> 00:13:55,477
That's the last of them.

275
00:13:55,560 --> 00:13:56,740
Oh, it's gorgeous!

276
00:13:57,340 --> 00:13:58,987
Golden bells and birds, huh?

277
00:13:59,480 --> 00:14:03,277
Do you think they might be symbols
of some ancient giant civilization?

278
00:14:03,360 --> 00:14:05,077
I really couldn't say for certain,

279
00:14:05,160 --> 00:14:08,077
but I'm pretty sure all these pieces
were part of the same casting.

280
00:14:08,160 --> 00:14:10,257
This creature is called a Southbird.

281
00:14:10,340 --> 00:14:12,697
You can still find them
on this island today.

282
00:14:12,780 --> 00:14:14,900
Wow, and they have a peculiar call?

283
00:14:15,060 --> 00:14:16,837
Uh-huh, just like in the logbook.

284
00:14:16,920 --> 00:14:21,391
Sailors have used Southbirds on ships
since ancient times to help them to...

285
00:14:23,800 --> 00:14:24,680
Oh no!

286
00:14:25,460 --> 00:14:26,290
What's wrong?

287
00:14:26,740 --> 00:14:27,797
This is really bad.

288
00:14:27,880 --> 00:14:30,877
You guys got to get to the forest on
the south side of the island right away.

289
00:14:30,960 --> 00:14:31,790
Huh?

290
00:14:31,873 --> 00:14:32,703
What do you mean?

291
00:14:32,786 --> 00:14:33,616
Are you crazy?

292
00:14:33,699 --> 00:14:34,529
Don't waste a minute.

293
00:14:34,612 --> 00:14:36,557
You got to capture one of
these birds and quickly.

294
00:14:36,640 --> 00:14:37,820
How come? Why?

295
00:14:37,920 --> 00:14:39,417
What do we need the bird for?

296
00:14:39,500 --> 00:14:40,760
Now listen carefully.

297
00:14:41,180 --> 00:14:43,257
Tomorrow you're going to
sail for the knock-up stream.

298
00:14:43,340 --> 00:14:45,057
It lies directly south of here.

299
00:14:45,140 --> 00:14:46,964
How are you going to get there?

300
00:14:49,260 --> 00:14:51,789
We just sail the ship
due south, of course.

301
00:14:52,060 --> 00:14:53,957
Have you forgotten this is the Grand Line?

302
00:14:54,040 --> 00:14:55,297
You're heading for open sea.

303
00:14:55,380 --> 00:14:57,017
You won't be able to get your bearings?

304
00:14:57,100 --> 00:14:58,597
Wait, he's right.

305
00:14:58,680 --> 00:15:00,457
Without a landmass to lock onto,

306
00:15:00,540 --> 00:15:03,397
the Log Pose will be useless and
there'll be no way for us to navigate.

307
00:15:03,480 --> 00:15:06,877
But there's got to be some way we can
figure out which direction to sail.

308
00:15:06,960 --> 00:15:10,372
There is, if you can lay your
hands on one of these birds.

309
00:15:10,740 --> 00:15:13,237
Certain animals are known to
have a very accurate internal

310
00:15:13,320 --> 00:15:16,097
sense of direction as if they're
born with a natural compass.

311
00:15:16,180 --> 00:15:19,517
That makes Zoro dumber than an animal.

312
00:15:19,600 --> 00:15:20,917
Oh, yeah. Right.

313
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:22,017
Like you have any room to...

314
00:15:22,100 --> 00:15:22,930
Talk.

315
00:15:23,013 --> 00:15:26,288
The South Bird is a perfect
example of this kind of creature.

316
00:15:27,520 --> 00:15:30,777
In the middle of a vast, barren
plain or a trackless ocean,

317
00:15:30,860 --> 00:15:33,742
its body will always point
in the same direction.

318
00:15:36,400 --> 00:15:37,230
You see?

319
00:15:37,313 --> 00:15:40,377
If you don't get going to that forest
and catch one of those birds right now,

320
00:15:40,460 --> 00:15:43,837
you'll have to kiss your only
chance to get to Sky Island goodbye.

321
00:15:43,920 --> 00:15:46,160
And you're just telling us this now?

322
00:15:46,740 --> 00:15:47,820
Quit your whining!

323
00:15:49,560 --> 00:15:51,540
There's no time for that now. Go!

324
00:15:51,820 --> 00:15:52,677
Move it or lose it!

325
00:15:52,760 --> 00:15:55,617
You expect us to go stumbling through
the forest in the middle of the night?

326
00:15:55,700 --> 00:15:56,818
Don't forget these.

327
00:15:58,540 --> 00:16:02,377
The three of us are gonna get started
on the repairs to your broken-down ship.

328
00:16:02,460 --> 00:16:05,697
You know, now that I think about it, we
probably shouldn't have had that party.

329
00:16:05,780 --> 00:16:06,610
Oh, sure!

330
00:16:06,693 --> 00:16:07,760
Now you tell us!

331
00:16:07,900 --> 00:16:09,497
Listen, the clock's ticking.

332
00:16:09,580 --> 00:16:10,977
You have until morning to catch a south

333
00:16:11,060 --> 00:16:13,707
bird or you can forget
about the whole thing.

334
00:16:14,160 --> 00:16:15,580
Whoa, it's pitch black.

335
00:16:15,780 --> 00:16:17,721
Will you let go of me, you idiot?

336
00:16:22,460 --> 00:16:24,977
My stomach's still so
stuffed from that dinner.

337
00:16:25,060 --> 00:16:27,280
I couldn't catch a bird if I saw one.

338
00:16:27,480 --> 00:16:28,640
So where are they?

339
00:16:29,960 --> 00:16:32,617
Let's just catch one of them
and get back to drinking.

340
00:16:32,700 --> 00:16:35,759
Will someone remind me how
we ended up in this mess?

341
00:16:36,540 --> 00:16:37,997
Nanny couldn't tell us in the daylight.

342
00:16:38,080 --> 00:16:40,237
It had to be in the middle of the night.

343
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:41,377
Where are the birds?

344
00:16:41,460 --> 00:16:43,217
If I had any idea where the birds were,

345
00:16:43,300 --> 00:16:44,877
do you think we'd be out
here looking for them?

346
00:16:44,960 --> 00:16:47,557
All we know is this thing's
got a peculiar call.

347
00:16:47,640 --> 00:16:50,337
Yeah, and it looks just like
that gold statue he showed us.

348
00:16:50,420 --> 00:16:51,917
It's kind of hard for
me to believe that any

349
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,037
bird could look that
screwy-looking in real life.

350
00:16:54,120 --> 00:16:56,837
Anyway, saying it has a
peculiar call is too vague.

351
00:16:56,920 --> 00:16:58,237
We can't ID it from that.

352
00:16:58,320 --> 00:17:02,673
Well, the old coot didn't seem to think
we'd have any trouble finding one.

353
00:17:03,500 --> 00:17:04,330
Hey!

354
00:17:04,413 --> 00:17:05,594
That sounded peculiar!

355
00:17:05,980 --> 00:17:07,580
Oh, you think? Yeah!

356
00:17:07,900 --> 00:17:08,900
Well, we're here.

357
00:17:09,080 --> 00:17:10,297
Let's do it and get it over with.

358
00:17:10,380 --> 00:17:11,637
Three nets, three teams.

359
00:17:11,720 --> 00:17:13,661
Let's split up and bag that bird.

360
00:17:13,840 --> 00:17:14,670
Right!

361
00:17:14,753 --> 00:17:15,583
Let's go!

362
00:17:15,666 --> 00:17:16,548
We're gonna beat up a bird!

363
00:17:18,100 --> 00:17:19,880
We're trying to catch it!

364
00:17:26,340 --> 00:17:27,693
Don't worry, Nami baby.

365
00:17:27,880 --> 00:17:30,357
No matter what we run into,
I'm here to protect you.

366
00:17:30,440 --> 00:17:31,560
Me too, Sanji Lump.

367
00:17:31,840 --> 00:17:34,237
Will you suck it up and
stop being such a wuss?

368
00:17:34,320 --> 00:17:36,117
Come on, guys, let's catch that bird.

369
00:17:36,200 --> 00:17:37,240
Get out of here.

370
00:17:38,160 --> 00:17:42,513
Okay, let's all settle down and see
if we can hear that bird's call again.

371
00:17:44,060 --> 00:17:45,860
Hey, there it is!

372
00:17:47,120 --> 00:17:49,300
Come on out! Moron!

373
00:17:50,960 --> 00:17:52,666
Hey, why are you catching me?

374
00:17:53,140 --> 00:17:55,360
Hey, nice accessorizing there, Nami.

375
00:17:55,480 --> 00:17:56,637
Where'd you get that thing?

376
00:17:56,720 --> 00:17:57,550
What thing?

377
00:17:59,800 --> 00:18:00,630
Spider!

378
00:18:02,720 --> 00:18:03,550
What?

379
00:18:03,633 --> 00:18:04,463
It's just a spider.

380
00:18:04,660 --> 00:18:06,189
Come here, you poor thing.

381
00:18:06,380 --> 00:18:08,160
I hate spiders, you idiot!

382
00:18:08,420 --> 00:18:09,714
Throw that thing away!

383
00:18:09,940 --> 00:18:11,417
Get rid of it, Usoppity!

384
00:18:11,500 --> 00:18:12,971
It's got to be poisonous!

385
00:18:13,500 --> 00:18:15,137
They're falling from the trees!

386
00:18:15,220 --> 00:18:16,980
This is just too nasty!

387
00:18:17,600 --> 00:18:19,718
I bet you're a species of tarantula.

388
00:18:19,880 --> 00:18:21,260
Don't worry, you guys.

389
00:18:21,460 --> 00:18:23,757
They don't bite you unless you
make them angry or frighten them.

390
00:18:23,840 --> 00:18:26,157
I used to wrestle with spiders
like this all the time.

391
00:18:26,240 --> 00:18:27,577
Are you out of your tiny little mind?

392
00:18:27,660 --> 00:18:30,217
Hairy bugs like that
completely freak me out!

393
00:18:30,300 --> 00:18:31,130
Yeah, me too.

394
00:18:31,213 --> 00:18:32,337
They make my flesh crawl.

395
00:18:32,420 --> 00:18:33,250
Throw it away!

396
00:18:33,333 --> 00:18:34,163
I can't stand it!

397
00:18:34,246 --> 00:18:35,076
Oh, really?

398
00:18:35,159 --> 00:18:36,777
But you don't seem to
have any problem with

399
00:18:36,860 --> 00:18:39,684
the centipedes and moths
on the tree behind you.

400
00:18:50,480 --> 00:18:51,310
Wait!

401
00:18:51,393 --> 00:18:52,520
Don't leave me here alone!

402
00:18:54,260 --> 00:18:55,090
Wait.

403
00:18:55,173 --> 00:18:56,597
That sounded like someone screaming.

404
00:18:56,680 --> 00:18:57,510
Really?

405
00:19:02,260 --> 00:19:03,220
A centipede.

406
00:19:03,460 --> 00:19:04,500
And a big one.

407
00:19:05,200 --> 00:19:07,577
You don't have to kill
every living thing we meet.

408
00:19:07,660 --> 00:19:08,719
It's kind of rude.

409
00:19:09,120 --> 00:19:11,767
Yeah, well, he shouldn't
have snuck up on me.

410
00:19:12,260 --> 00:19:16,280
Hey, wait now what you're
going back the way we came

411
00:19:19,240 --> 00:19:24,860
There it is again, come on and be
careful not to step in the quicksand

412
00:19:26,960 --> 00:19:28,320
Hey wait for

413
00:19:33,360 --> 00:19:34,240
I gotcha!

414
00:19:35,600 --> 00:19:36,640
It's an Atlas!

415
00:19:37,300 --> 00:19:38,620
Atlas? That's right,

416
00:19:38,840 --> 00:19:40,637
this one and the Hercules
beetle are some of the

417
00:19:40,720 --> 00:19:43,277
most prized insects in
the world for collectors.

418
00:19:43,360 --> 00:19:45,437
You mean there are people
who collect insects?

419
00:19:45,520 --> 00:19:48,077
Oh yeah, and these guys are
some of the hardest to catch.

420
00:19:48,160 --> 00:19:51,219
Hey, which one is cooler,
that bug or the One Piece?

421
00:19:51,600 --> 00:19:52,820
That's a tough one.

422
00:19:53,160 --> 00:19:55,120
Luffy, there's one over here too!

423
00:19:55,460 --> 00:19:57,640
Oh yeah, meow, my stab beetle! Now!

424
00:19:57,920 --> 00:19:58,900
Gotta catch it!

425
00:20:00,320 --> 00:20:01,580
He flew away!

426
00:20:05,600 --> 00:20:06,430
After him!

427
00:20:06,560 --> 00:20:07,913
Don't let him get away!

428
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:10,720
A beehive! Run!

429
00:20:23,800 --> 00:20:26,180
What the heck is wrong with this forage?

430
00:20:27,780 --> 00:20:29,020
Luffy, look up there!

431
00:20:29,700 --> 00:20:30,530
The bird!

432
00:20:30,613 --> 00:20:32,755
He's the one who dropped the beehives!

433
00:20:34,260 --> 00:20:35,090
What'd he say?

434
00:20:35,180 --> 00:20:36,010
What'd he say?

435
00:20:36,093 --> 00:20:37,233
Did you understand him?

436
00:20:38,320 --> 00:20:41,620
He said, I'll kill anyone
who disturbs this forest!

437
00:20:53,480 --> 00:20:57,737
now now little boy you don't really
want to go and make us angry do you it's

438
00:20:57,820 --> 00:21:01,317
not nice to show up in the middle of
the night and demand gold from someone

439
00:21:01,400 --> 00:21:05,937
now get lost we've got a lot of work
to do and not a lot of time to do it

440
00:21:06,020 --> 00:21:11,800
you must be the bodyguards and the
old man must be the ex-pirate cricket.

441
00:21:12,100 --> 00:21:15,737
I heard the sad story of the
Montblanc family back at the tavern.

442
00:21:15,820 --> 00:21:17,420
It moved me to tears.

443
00:21:17,880 --> 00:21:21,140
But stealing another man's
treasure moves me even more.

444
00:21:21,320 --> 00:21:22,300
I'm a pirate.

445
00:21:23,720 --> 00:21:24,550
Cricket!

446
00:21:24,633 --> 00:21:28,057
Maybe you'd like to know what people call
me before I take your whole life's work.

447
00:21:28,140 --> 00:21:28,970
The hyena!
